## 5. The two-stage prediction pipeline (RouteNet-Fermi's main stage)

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    H["historical telemetry / demand"] --> S1["① time-series forecaster (HAS a time axis)<br/>naive+exog / SARIMAX / darts"]
    S1 --> TM["future traffic matrix @ t+k<br/>{p50, p90, p95, p99}"]
    TM --> S2["② ★PerformanceModel (NO time axis · steady-state)★<br/>make_performance_model(gnn | analytic)<br/>run predict() once per demand quantile"]
    S2 --> KPI["future KPI distribution @ t+k<br/>{delay, jitter, loss, link load}"]
    KPI --> S3["③ breach.py — survival-function interpolation"]
    S3 --> PR["P(load &gt; 85%) and P(delay &gt; SLA)"]
    PR --> S4["④ intent.py"]
    S4 --> INC["Incident(source=prediction)<br/>&quot;core2-core4 will breach in ~140 ticks (~11.7 h), prob 0.86&quot;"]
```

**Three invariants (talking points):**
- Time comes **only** from ①. RouteNet-Fermi in ② does a **steady-state map only** — it does not forecast time.
- Link load is **derived inside** ② from the traffic matrix (`load = Σ traffic / capacity`) — **not** predicted.
- Whether ② is `analytic` or `gnn` is **transparent** to everything above — that is the whole point of the seam.

> ⚠️ **Two different "gnn"s — do not conflate:**
> **RouteNet-Fermi = the *performance model*** (`perf/routenet/`) — ✅ implemented, trained, wired into 3 places.
> **`GnnPredictor` = a *time-series forecaster*** (`predictor.py`) — ❌ a deliberate empty seam (RouteNet-Fermi has no time axis, so it is *not* a forecaster).

